# Half-Life HD tripmine. Measured by vrcardgen, carded by hand.
#
# Part J: "Hold it to a wall: a ghost shows where it will sit. Press it to
# the surface and it attaches there, oriented to the surface, and arms."
#
# ONE CARD, THREE GAME DIRECTORIES. valve_hd, gearbox_hd and bshift_hd each
# ship their own v_tripmine.mdl and the three files are NOT identical -
# 178716, 217200 and 230952 bytes, three different checksums, 793 / 799 / 771
# vertices. What they share is the rig AND the mine: 48 bones, 9 sequences,
# bone-name hash 0x19c33a89 in all three, measured, and a `tripmine` bone
# whose 483 vertices measure the same box to the hundredth in every file
# (22.33 x 10.97 x 10.15). What differs is only the arms - the hands submodel
# is V_Gordon_hands_reference (310 verts), V_soldier_hands_reference (316) and
# V_barney_hands_reference (288). Binding is by fingerprint, so one card
# serves all three.
#
# The SD rigs are different weapons as far as a card is concerned and decline
# this card, which is right: retail valve is 46 bones / 0xe8898805, gearbox 45
# / 0x8bdfc00b, bshift 44 / 0xae61d519. All four agree with CENSUS.txt.

weapon v_tripmine
match  bones 48  seqs 9  hash 0x19c33a89

# NOTHING ON IT MOVES, AND THAT IS THE MEASUREMENT, NOT AN ASSUMPTION.
# vrcardgen offers ZERO joint candidates on this rig, and the reason is that
# there is only one weapon bone. Of the 48 bones, 47 are the arms: `Bip01`
# and its spine, two clavicles, two upper arms, two forearms, two hands,
# thirty finger bones and L_Arm_bone / R_Arm_bone (63 vertices each, 7.9% of
# the mesh apiece - the sleeves). The forty-eighth is `tripmine`, root-
# parented, 483 vertices, 60.9% of the mesh. There is no second part to be a
# joint: no pin, no lever, no cover, no arming switch, no leg.
#
# Its measured motion is the viewmodel being carried, not a part working.
# Posing the mine's own origin through all nine sequences: 17.69 units in
# `holster` and 16.87 in `draw` (being stowed and raised), 2.14 of sway in
# `fidget`, 0.67 in idle1/idle2/arm1, and EXACTLY 0.000 in `place`. A part
# that only ever moves when the whole weapon moves is the weapon.

# `thrown` IS THE PRESET AND IT NAMES THIS WEAPON. vr_gun.cpp:159-166: "NO
# JOINTS AND NO FUSE BY DEFAULT. Most thrown things have neither: a satchel is
# thrown or set down, A TRIPMINE IS PRESSED TO A WALL, a snark is let go."
# vr_feed.cpp:126 names it again - "a satchel, a tripmine and a snark are
# thrown or placed; only a grenade has a pin between the player and the thing
# happening" - so with no `pin_clear` point declared here, VRFeed_Init hands
# the mine over with pin_pulled already 1 and chamber VRR_LIVE. The whole
# mechanism is RELEASE AGAINST A SURFACE, and finding the surface is the
# world's job (Part I), not the weapon's. Nothing of the preset is overridden.
type   thrown

# NO FUSE, SAID OUT LOUD. vr_feed.cpp:525 is explicit about which weapon this
# line is for: "NO FUSE, NOTHING TO BURN. A satchel waits for its radio, A
# TRIPMINE ARMS WHERE IT IS PUT, and a snark has opinions of its own."
#
# The 2.5 seconds in dlls/tripmine.cpp:124 are NOT this number. That clock
# belongs to CTripmineGrenade - the entity the server spawns 8 units off the
# surface - and it is already run there, by PowerupThink, after the mine has
# left the hand. `fuse_ms` is the HELD weapon's fuse, the grenade's three
# seconds from the pin. A mine in the hand is never burning, so this is 0 and
# VRFeed_Time returns immediately on it.
fuse   0           # it arms where it is placed

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS MEASURED HERE AND NOT DECLARED, because the format has nowhere to
# put it. These numbers are the reason the two lines above are the right two
# lines, and the next person should not have to re-pose the model to get them.
#
# TWO HANDS, ONE AT EACH END. Posing `place` and reading both hand bones in
# the mine body's own frame, the grip is symmetric and fixed:
#
#   Bip01 L Hand  ( 5.74, -0.42, -4.74)
#   Bip01 R Hand  (-5.93, -0.41, -4.63)      11.67 units apart
#
# and the viewmodel mine submodel spans x -5.57 .. 5.59, so the hands sit
# exactly on its two ends with the 11.16-unit block bridged between them.
# This is a two-handed object held flat, not a grip and a fore-end. Part F
# wants those as its two hand constraints; the card format has no keyword for
# a bare grip point (`grab` hangs off a joint, and there is no joint), so they
# are recorded here rather than invented into a joint that does not exist.
#
# THE PRESS, MEASURED. `place` is 11 frames and the mine body never moves in
# any of them, so the whole gesture is in the arms. The hand midpoint, in
# view space:
#
#   f0  (15.76, -0.09,  -9.95)   the mine held up, arms in
#   f6  (21.32,  0.00, -12.46)   5.56 units FORWARD, 2.51 down - the press
#   f10 (12.54,  0.00, -22.33)   8.78 back and 9.87 down - hands withdrawn
#
# Six frames of push and four of letting go. That is Part J's "press it to the
# surface" with a number on it: about 5.6 units of forward travel at the end
# of the arms. It is not a joint travel and it is not in `match`, because
# nothing on the weapon moves through it - it is how far the PLAYER moves.
# Stock Half-Life traces 128 units forward from the eye (tripmine.cpp:463) and
# refuses to place if nothing is hit; in VR the hand's own reach replaces that
# trace, which is why the gesture matters and the rate does not.
#
# NO `fire` LINE, SO NO RPM. tripmine.cpp:505 sets a 0.3 s next-attack, and
# that is exactly the invisible second timer a hand-worked weapon does not
# want: the limiter should be reaching out and touching a wall. The preset's
# semi-only default trigger is never consulted on a thrown weapon.
#
# `arm1` IS A DUPLICATE OF `idle1` AND NEITHER ARM SEQUENCE IS EVER PLAYED.
# Compared channel by channel - 48 bones x 6 channels x 46 frames - `arm1`
# (animindex 13364) and `idle1` (animindex 5844) differ in ZERO samples, max
# deviation 0.000000. And TRIPMINE_ARM1 / TRIPMINE_ARM2 appear nowhere in the
# game but the two enums themselves (dlls/tripmine.cpp:32 and
# cl_dll/ev_hldm.cpp:1597); PrimaryAttack sends no animation at all and the
# client event plays TRIPMINE_DRAW. So sequence 3, which the model labels
# `place` where the enum calls it ARM2, is dead content in stock Half-Life -
# an 11-frame placing animation nobody ever sees. It is the only sequence
# that shows the mechanism, and measuring it is what this card did with it.
#
# TWO BODYGROUPS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS WHY vrcardgen SAYS 29.7. The model
# carries `hands` (reference / blank) and `mine` (V_tripmine_reference 372
# verts / w_tripmine_Reference 111 verts), which is what CTripmineGrenade's
# `pev->body = 3` selects - blank hands plus the world mine. Both mine
# submodels hang off the one `tripmine` bone, and the world one is a clean
# 2:1 scaled copy of the viewmodel one (22.33 x 10.97 x 10.15 against
# 11.16 x 5.48 x 5.07, same centroid x). vrcardgen's "weapon extent 29.7
# units" is the union of both plus the arms. THE MINE A HAND ACTUALLY HOLDS
# IS 11.16 x 5.48 x 5.07, diagonal 13.43.
#
# NO ATTACHMENTS AT ALL, which is correct: a tripmine has no muzzle and is
# not fired. There was no muzzle to measure and none is declared.
#
# NO CONTROL IS DECLARED. Every name in the parser's control vocabulary is a
# catch - slide_lock, mag_latch, sear, spoon (vr_card.cpp:260-267) - and a
# control naming a catch no joint has is refused outright, with `spoon` the
# single exception. A tripmine has no spoon: its safety is the 2.5-second
# power-up on the placed entity, which is the server's and not the hand's.
#
# NO STRIKE LINE, so the parser's weak pistol-whip default stands - radius 2,
# counting from 60 units/sec, full at 260 (vr_strike.cpp:16-18). Part J gives
# the butt-stroke to firearms; a tripmine is not one, and nobody has measured
# what an 11-unit slab of shaped charge does to a houndeye when swung.
#
# MESH. Not solid, in any of the three: valve_hd 1465 triangles with 4.7% of
# edges open, gearbox_hd 1416 and 6.6%, bshift_hd 1430 and 4.3%, with 28
# non-manifold edges apiece. "Mostly closed" by mesh_completeness, which makes
# it one of the better retail viewmodels - but still open, like 69 of the 101.
# Nothing here needs a closed mesh; do not assume one.
